WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:05.620
Actor/Filmmaker Adam Bradley: When I was getting started in the film
industry somebody shared a phrase with

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:14.020
me that has stuck with me for the last
23 years. Fast, cheap and good: pick two. My

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:20.169
name is Adam Bradley and in 2012 I wrote,
produced, acted in and edited a no-budget

00:00:20.169 --> 00:00:25.119
feature film called Yesterday Last Year.
Yesterday Last Year is a mumblecore

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:28.989
sci-fi film about a love triangle that
gets more complicated once a

00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:33.489
time-machine enters the picture we shot
it in nine days with three actors in one

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:38.350
location for 3500 bucks a couple of
months ago it was released on Amazon

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.760
Prime and Vimeo on demand so in this
video what I want to do is share my

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.989
experiences the lessons that I learned
and the mistakes that I made creating

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:51.579
this work there's a lot of information
out there about making your own work but

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.210
not enough people talk about one of the
central challenges involved in making

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:59.620
your own work which I believe is this
unless you have a lot of money to spend

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.269
you're going to be the one doing a lot
of that work and when you are writing

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:10.600
and producing and acting and editing and
maybe even directing additional

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.790
challenges come into play
for me everything boils down to one key

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:21.070
rule once you're actually on set you're
going to be under the gun and everything

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:26.560
has to be done fast which means now you
have to choose between cheap and good if

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.950
you've spent your time in development
and in pre-production planning properly

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.939
you can get a lot of quality for not
very much money here are some of the

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:40.479
things that I've learned first you have
to have partners and you have to have

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.020
partners who are in it for the long haul
you got to have people who are right

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.500
there with you on day one and who will
be there all the way through til the

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.689
bitter bitter end but you need to pick
those people carefully you have to trust

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:57.430
them and you have to like them you have
to be able to disagree with them and

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:01.719
resolve those disagreements without
throwing shit at each other and in

00:02:01.719 --> 00:02:07.630
addition to that you want skillsets to
complement each other in my experience

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.629
there are people who are really good at
making things happen and then there are

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:13.989
people who are really good at getting
things done people who can make things

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:16.000
happen these are the sort of people who
can

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.330
walk into a luxury hotel asked to speak
to the manager and 15 minutes later

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.080
you've got yourself a suite and use of
the rooftop bar once it closes for the

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.130
night people who get things done these
are the sort of people who will pay

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.950
attention to every single dollar and
where it's going who know what the

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.630
actors schedules are who know who has
what sort of dietary restriction who can

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.390
look at the schedule and say yeah this
makes sense no we need to move this

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.280
scene over here you need both of those
skill sets and if you're writing

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.530
something because you also want to
direct and produce it then you need two

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.489
other partners who are going to handle
those details while you're busy

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:57.580
directing or acting in your project the
nice thing is people who make things

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.870
happen when you're shooting they become
producers of marketing and distribution

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.810
right get them thinking about the big
picture get them thinking about sales

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.290
get them thinking about distribution get
them thinking about festivals so they

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:14.050
should be heading up behind the scenes
footage photographs interviews all of

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.510
that stuff the people who are good at
getting things done they become the

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.470
production managers and so while you're
on set directing or acting they're

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.640
overseeing the logistical details of the
next location you're going to or what's

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.190
coming up for tomorrow because you can't
be doing both when you're writing right

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:37.150
for what you have available and
yesterday last year the whole thing is

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:41.739
set in a garage and when the director
came on board he had his own garage

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:46.630
which was fantastic
great problem solved except that then he

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.500
dropped out so we lost that location but
that's fine because our production

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.360
designer he had a workshop so great a
garage and a workshop they look

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.970
basically the same we're gonna shoot in
his workshop

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:02.620
except that then two weeks before we
started shooting his mom scheduled a big

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.769
family vacation so he had to leave and
we lost that location as well four days

00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:12.190
before were scheduled to go to camera
I'm walking around in my neighborhood

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:16.630
trying to find a garage and everybody
that had one we was either too small or

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:20.650
it had too much stuff in it or they
wanted too much money but fortunately

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.090
our director Jeff Hanley and our first
ad Yousef Audrey they found a garage

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.980
that was owned by a couple of ex film
students who were quite happy to let

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.090
shoot there and more importantly they'll
shoot there let they let us borrow their

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.990
bathroom they let us borrow their
kitchen and they let us use their power

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.350
but this came about because I wrote
something that required a specific

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:48.250
location that I didn't have access to
and it almost ended the project you want

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.640
to do an early read-through when the
script is as good as you can get it on

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.420
your own get some friends over get some
people that you trust and have them read

00:04:55.420 --> 00:05:00.250
it out and have them read it out word
for word you want to hear what your

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.050
language sounds like on the page and
then ask them questions and not

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.080
questions like did you like it did you
not like it what did you like what did

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.820
you hate but more specific questions
when were you engaged when did you first

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.800
get hooked into this story when were you
bored did anything happen that popped

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.730
you out of the story did anything happen
that confused you did anything happen

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:25.170
that made you feel like yeah more more
and more give me more of that please

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.210
those are the sorts of questions that
are gonna let you know when your

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:35.560
audience is engaged and when they're not
then once you've cast the film you want

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.280
to do a second read-through with the
actual actors who are going to be

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.170
playing the roles and this time let them
paraphrase if they want to the script is

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:47.320
there you can always go back to the
script and insist that they do it as

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:52.090
written but if you let your actors play
around and you let them paraphrase they

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:58.840
may come up with a phrase with a word
with a way of expressing an idea that

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.420
you hadn't thought of that's actually
better than what you had thought of

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.350
because once you've cast it these are
the actors who are going to be bringing

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:10.420
your characters to life for better for
worse they are the characters and if

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.810
they have a slightly different take on
how this character might say something

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.550
give it a shot you really don't have
anything to lose okay so now I want to

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.070
talk about some of the things that I
learned about production when you're

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:26.410
making your own work and playing more
than one role the bottom line is no

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.580
matter what you're doing on set whether
you're acting or directing or producing

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:35.800
you have to be flexible you have to be
willing to take that plan that you've

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:42.040
spent months coming up with and throw it
out the window it is a lot easier to

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.090
shift from one clear plan
- another clear plan than it is to walk

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:50.229
onto set with no plan or a vague plan
and try and come up with a clearer idea

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:56.259
right then so the plan that you spent
months coming up with wasn't wasted it

00:06:56.259 --> 00:07:01.990
has set you up to be able to improvise
that's the purpose of the plan if you're

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:07.870
acting show up off-book and show up
off-book prepared to jump into any scene

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.080
at a moment's notice this is
particularly helpful if you're only

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.599
shooting in one or two locations because
we might need to jump to scene 12 and

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:23.860
then to scene four because hey Scott was
late or Amy had to leave early never

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.120
ever ever yell cut if you're the actor
and you make a mistake figure out a way

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.530
to keep going if you're a director and
you're actors make a mistake make them

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.900
keep going
don't yell cut as soon as somebody

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:39.190
screws up a line force them to figure it
out there's nothing more exciting than

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:43.509
watching somebody on camera actually
figure out how to solve a problem this

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:47.680
scene is my favorite scene in all of
yesterday last year you went back and

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.419
then came right back here how do I do
that well the Machine brought you back

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:56.650
you can do that you said it did know
according to this it's a one-way trip I

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.940
mean it's not logically impossible that
you could do a return but the way it's

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:07.330
set up here that would leave you with it
with no number there the line as written

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:15.969
is according to this it's a one-way trip
it's not logically impossible to do a

00:08:15.969 --> 00:08:21.569
return but then you'd end up with an
imaginary number here which is nonsense

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:27.960
that's what the script says Scott gets
partway through and loses the line and

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:34.779
stares at the board hoping praying that
it would come to him it doesn't so he

00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:41.140
spits out something that's basically
like what the line is I'm standing right

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:46.390
next to him and what I'm thinking is oh
shit he's lost his line how can I help

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:54.190
all right I'm just gonna say my next
line how did you get back here and I say

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.440
it at the exact time that Scott figures
out what he's supposed

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.370
say next and so those two lines overlap
and then we stop and we stare at each

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.940
other and there's this moment of are you
gonna talk am I gonna talk how are we

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.050
gonna this is not the way it's written I
mean it's not logically impossible that

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.640
you could do a return but the way it's
set up here that would leave you with it

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:19.610
with no number there well then how'd you
get here in arsons well you almost leave

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:27.080
your Donald that only happened because
neither Scott nor I pulled the ripcord

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:32.060
we both stayed in the scene we both kept
working and Jeff the director he didn't

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:37.160
yell cut he let us figure it out so
whether you're acting or whether you're

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:43.130
directing don't let your mistakes throw
you figure out a way to keep moving and

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:47.990
move through them because you can always
do another take as soon as the camera

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:53.660
starts running you're on I can't tell
you the number of times that we got a

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.310
look or a shift or a brief little
movement that made it into the final

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:02.660
film and if you actually listen to the
audio of that take and the scene hadn't

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:08.390
started at that point but it's this
beautiful honest simple moment that is

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:12.320
in the movie that footage is golden and
you're gonna want that footage when you

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:18.020
get into the editing room don't schedule
your most important stuff at the top or

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:24.080
end of the day if you're not paying
people you can't guarantee that you're

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:27.950
gonna get exactly the same crew every
single day so it may shift it may change

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:32.390
you'll probably have a core group of
people that show up all the time but

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:35.930
then you may get a different grip you
may get a different script supervisor

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:41.150
you may have a different boom operator
top of the day people are still figuring

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:48.380
out how this crew works how this set
works if you put the most important

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.360
stuff up first it's going to be a little
wonky and nobody's really in the groove

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:58.610
yet no one's had their coffee nobody's
fed so start with something fairly easy

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:02.120
so that you can bang it out get some
momentum going everybody feels really

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.850
good because hey we've been here now and
we've already got three shots off

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:09.710
fantastic don't schedule it for the end
of the day either because I guarantee

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.030
you're gonna get to the end of the day
you

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:14.270
gonna be under crunch you won't have
enough time to do it properly and then

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:21.320
your most important scene is going to
suffer on the same note because your

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:26.390
crew may shift and change from day to
day know what the key positions are

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:32.120
where you need a continuous presence
that is likely going to change with

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:38.420
every project you wouldn't think that
having a different script supervisor

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:44.690
every day would be so problematic but
when you're making a movie about time

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:49.220
travel and when you're shooting it out
of order if you look at this scene and

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:54.890
you compare the tighter shot to the
wider shot if you look really closely

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:59.860
you can see that my shirt is different
now we punched in to try and cover that

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:04.970
but we shot these on two different days
and nobody was playing close enough

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:08.450
attention to what I was wearing because
we didn't have a dedicated wardrobe

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:13.160
person and because we had a rotating
script supervisor we had three different

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.850
script supervisors because we couldn't
pay one person to show up every single

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:23.270
day shoot on the highest resolution you
can afford we were originally gonna

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:30.170
shoot this movie on DSLRs and then about
two weeks before production one of our

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:35.030
gaffers said hey I know a guy who has a
red and he's willing to rent it to you

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:40.850
the entire package for 800 bucks I can't
tell you how many times that saved our

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:45.170
ass in post-production because we had
all of this additional resolution that

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:50.450
we could punch into and make singles
we're originally there was a two-shot a

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:55.600
dolly in when actually it was static we
were able to do that digitally in post

00:12:55.600 --> 00:13:00.860
because we had this 4k footage that we
could punch into one of the things that

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:08.170
I am most thankful to Jeff our director
for is he said look let's not rush this

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:14.420
we don't need to get this done by 2014
what we need to do is make this as good

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:22.220
as we possibly can because we only get
one shot so use your purpose use your

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.800
vision to figure out which festivals
your

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:29.600
four and then work your way backwards
don't look at their final deadline

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.569
because by their final deadline they've
probably already started to make

00:13:32.569 --> 00:13:37.459
decisions and slot some movies in so
look at their early deadline and maybe

00:13:37.459 --> 00:13:42.589
it's not their early deadline 2020
because if that's three months away you

00:13:42.589 --> 00:13:48.290
do not want to rush through your post in
three months early deadline 2021 you're

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:52.429
much better off taking that extra year
and making your movie as good as it

00:13:52.429 --> 00:13:58.879
possibly can be yesterday last year took
four years to go through post some of

00:13:58.879 --> 00:14:03.429
that was just syncing up the Ford
separate audio tracks with the video

00:14:03.429 --> 00:14:08.059
some of it came about because we cut the
whole thing together in a linear

00:14:08.059 --> 00:14:12.410
chronological way which is how it was
written and then when we got to picture

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:18.170
lock Jeff said you know this movies
about time travel why we don't need to

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:21.860
tell it in a linear way let's tell it
out of order let's start with scene four

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:28.369
and go for five six and then loop back
to scene one okay so we did that and we

00:14:28.369 --> 00:14:33.529
spent almost another year re cutting the
film reorganizing a tree cutting the

00:14:33.529 --> 00:14:36.829
scenes that we were both really really
happy with it and excited this was sort

00:14:36.829 --> 00:14:42.619
of new and exciting and then we got to
picture lock and we screened it for a

00:14:42.619 --> 00:14:48.860
couple of people and then editor friend
said look it's really interesting and I

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:54.949
enjoyed watching it but I spent most of
my time trying to figure out what the

00:14:54.949 --> 00:15:00.319
sequence of events was whether this
scene comes before or after the scene

00:15:00.319 --> 00:15:04.579
that I just saw and so what I'm not
paying attention to is the love triangle

00:15:04.579 --> 00:15:08.660
I'm not tracking the emotional
entanglements of the three characters

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:14.869
and as soon as he said that Jeff and I
both went yeah shit because that's the

00:15:14.869 --> 00:15:18.619
core of the movie right this film really
is about a love triangle it's about

00:15:18.619 --> 00:15:23.179
betrayal
and forgiveness the time machine is just

00:15:23.179 --> 00:15:28.699
there as a complicating device to make
things worse for the characters once the

00:15:28.699 --> 00:15:37.220
movie becomes about the time travel it's
no longer really being successful so we

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:40.790
went back to the draw
we reorganized everything back into

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:45.920
linear fashion we took another six
months to put it back lock the picture

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:52.100
and then cleaning up the audio took a
year simultaneously we were getting the

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:58.460
score done that took about a year but we
took that time because the movie is much

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:04.420
better now than it was in its first
incarnation making a movie on a

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:09.410
well-organised film set is an amazing
experience there's nothing else like it

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:13.250
in the world when the whole team comes
together and everybody knows what story

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:17.330
they're making and why they're making it
and what they're doing and you're firing

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:21.740
on all cylinders because everything's
really well prepared and the crew is

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.550
being taken care of and the actors are
bringing their a-game and the directors

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:28.900
are bringing their a-game and the
producers have all their shit together

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:37.040
there's nothing like that in the world
so go out plan your shoot properly so

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:41.120
that when you get into the thick of it
you can throw everything to the wind and

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:47.080
improvise I'm Adam Bradley thanks for
watching

